theory RHTNLocalExchange
/*
================================================================================
  RHTN local device-to-device exchange -- symbolic analysis (Tamarin)
================================================================================

WHICH TREE THIS IS.  `local/`, a third tree, and it needed to be one.
`wire-only/` holds properties a third party checks from bytes it receives;
there is no third party here and no record is produced.  `compliant/` holds
obligations only the acting party can check; this is not an obligation but an
exchange under an adversary.  What `local/` holds is a **two-party exchange
across two channels of different strength** (`wire-format.md` §14.4), and the
question is which of its guarantees come from the bytes and which come from
the physical premise.

WHAT IS MODELLED.  §14.4's exchange, to the point where a bearer-carried
message is accepted:

  1. each device shows its 16-byte contribution optically;
  2. both derive the ceremony-id, SHA-256 over the tag and the two
     contributions (design §13.2), and each shows it;
  3. each checks the other's ceremony-id against its own -- §14.4.1's
     "a mismatch is where a man in the middle shows";
  4. a bearer carries an anchored message, whose anchor and echoed
     contribution the receiver checks against what it read optically
     (§14.4.2).

  ONE RULE PAIR STANDS FOR ALL THREE ANCHORED MESSAGES.  `IntentExchange`,
  `ProximityOutcomes` and `CandidateHandover` differ in payload and share
  their entire checking discipline -- anchor against own ceremony-id, and for
  the intent the echoed contribution as well.  The payload is opaque here, so
  the result covers all three.  `DeviceIntroduction` and `DeviceCredential`
  are NOT modelled: §14.4.3 states outright that they carry no anchor, so
  there is nothing for a symbolic model to check that the section does not
  already concede.

THE TWO CHANNELS, and this is the whole of the theory's content.

  OPTICAL: **authentic and public.**  A `!Optical` fact is created only by the
  rules below, so the adversary cannot inject one -- that is §14.1's
  non-intermediability, taken as the premise it is.  Every optical value is
  also sent to `Out`, so the adversary reads all of them -- that is §14.4.1's
  "neither is secret", stated as plainly in the model as in the document.

  BEARER: **fully adversarial.**  `Out` and `In`, nothing else.  Bluetooth, a
  local radio, or a fetch through FuerosNet: §14.4 leaves the choice to the
  shell and ranks it by locality rather than integrity, so the model gives the
  adversary the strongest position the ranking permits.

WHAT IS ESTABLISHED, AND WHAT IS NOT.  The one positive property is
`an_accepted_anchor_was_derived_by_both`: a message accepted over the bearer
carries a ceremony-id both devices independently computed and each read off
the other's screen.  It is mutation-tested -- delete the anchor comparison and
it falsifies.

  **The anchor does not authenticate the sender, and does not prevent replay
  into another ceremony.**  Both are recorded below as `exists-trace` lemmas
  that VERIFY, because the attack being reachable is the finding: the anchor
  and the contributions are public by §14.4.1, so an adversary that has read
  the screens can wrap any payload it likes in a valid-looking anchor.  What
  the check buys is that a payload cannot be accepted under an anchor nobody
  derived -- ceremony-binding against a party who did not watch, and nothing
  against one who did.

  WHY THAT IS NOT A DEFECT IN THE DESIGN, and why the model cannot say so
  itself.  §14.4.1 puts the man-in-the-middle resistance in one sentence --
  "each party reads both values off a screen it is looking at" -- and the cost
  of reading those screens is physical presence, which is the cost design §1
  meters.  **Dolev-Yao has no locality**: reading `Out` is free here, so the
  adversary of this model is automatically co-present at no cost, and the
  protection the design relies on is outside what any symbolic model can
  express.  The lemmas therefore mark the boundary rather than a break: past
  it the guarantee is physical, and §14.1 already states it as a goal that
  nothing can verify.

LIMITS, stated as the ceremony theories state theirs.
  - **The contribution ordering is a projection.**  design §13.2 orders the
    two contributions by ascending participant keyhash; the model fixes the
    initiator's first.  That is what the ordering rule achieves -- one
    canonical term both sides compute -- and an implementation getting the
    order wrong produces two different ceremony-ids, which is the mismatch
    step 3 catches and not a property this theory adds.
  - **The payload is opaque.**  Nothing here models what an intent's bundle
    is worth: a bundle entry's integrity comes from its own signatures
    (`wire-format.md` §5.3 requires each to verify alone), not from the
    anchor, so bearer substitution cannot manufacture a qualifying record it
    does not already hold.  That is the wire theories' subject, not this one's.
  - **No confidentiality is modelled or claimed**, because none is wanted:
    §14.4.2 closed the confidential-bearer question, nothing carried being
    secret.
================================================================================
*/

begin

builtins: hashing

restriction Distinct:
  "All a b #i. Distinct(a, b) @ #i ==> not(a = b)"

restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE OPTICAL EXCHANGE -- authentic, and public
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Each rule mints one contribution and shows it.  The `!Optical` fact is the
  screen: unforgeable, because no adversary rule produces one.  The `Out` is
  the same value in the clear, because a QR held up between two phones hides
  nothing from a third camera.
*/

rule Initiator_Shows_Contribution:
    [ Fr(~ca) ]
  --[ Showed($A, $B, ~ca), Distinct($A, $B) ]->
    [ !Optical($A, $B, 'contribution', ~ca)
    , Out(~ca)
    , Init1($A, $B, ~ca) ]

rule Responder_Shows_Contribution:
    [ Fr(~cb) ]
  --[ Showed($B, $A, ~cb), Distinct($B, $A) ]->
    [ !Optical($B, $A, 'contribution', ~cb)
    , Out(~cb)
    , Resp1($B, $A, ~cb) ]

/*
  THE CEREMONY-ID, derived and then shown (design §13.2, §14.4.1).  Both
  rules build the identical term -- the initiator's contribution first -- for
  the reason the document orders by keyhash: the two devices must compute one
  value, not two.
*/

rule Initiator_Confirms:
    [ Init1($A, $B, ca), !Optical($B, $A, 'contribution', cb) ]
  --[ Derived($A, $B, h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>)) ]->
    [ !Optical($A, $B, 'confirm', h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>))
    , Out(h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>))
    , Init2($A, $B, ca, cb, h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>)) ]

rule Responder_Confirms:
    [ Resp1($B, $A, cb), !Optical($A, $B, 'contribution', ca) ]
  --[ Derived($B, $A, h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>)) ]->
    [ !Optical($B, $A, 'confirm', h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>))
    , Out(h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>))
    , Resp2($B, $A, cb, ca, h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>)) ]

/*
  THE CHECK THAT ANCHORS.  Each device reads the other's ceremony-id and
  compares it with its own; a mismatch stops the ceremony (§14.4.1), which
  here is the `Eq` restriction refusing the trace.
*/

rule Initiator_Anchors:
    [ Init2($A, $B, ca, cb, cid), !Optical($B, $A, 'confirm', theirs) ]
  --[ Eq(theirs, cid), Anchored($A, $B, cid) ]->
    [ AnchorA($A, $B, ca, cb, cid) ]

rule Responder_Anchors:
    [ Resp2($B, $A, cb, ca, cid), !Optical($A, $B, 'confirm', theirs) ]
  --[ Eq(theirs, cid), Anchored($B, $A, cid) ]->
    [ AnchorB($B, $A, cb, ca, cid) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE BEARER -- everything the adversary holds
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  One send and one take, standing for all three anchored messages.  The
  payload is fresh on the honest path and any term the adversary likes on the
  other, which is exactly the substitution §14.4.2's echo check is there to
  catch.
*/

rule Bearer_Send:
    [ AnchorA($A, $B, ca, cb, cid), Fr(~payload) ]
  --[ Sent($A, $B, cid, ~payload) ]->
    [ Out(<'anchored', cid, ca, ~payload>) ]

rule Bearer_Take:
    [ AnchorB($B, $A, cb, ca, cid), In(<'anchored', anchor, echo, payload>) ]
  --[ Eq(anchor, cid)                 // the anchor, against this device's own
    , Eq(echo, ca)                    // the echoed contribution (§14.4.2)
    // THE ANCHOR AS THE MESSAGE CARRIED IT, not as this device computed it.
    // Recording the device's own `cid` here would make the lemma below hold
    // through `Responder_Anchors`' optical confirm whatever the bearer said,
    // and deleting the comparison above would leave it verifying -- a green
    // lemma for an unchecked field, which is the failure the theory
    // mutations exist to catch.
    , Accepted($B, $A, anchor, payload) ]->
    [ ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LEMMAS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

// SANITY, and it must exclude the adversary doing the work: an honest run in
// which the payload accepted is the payload sent.
lemma exchange_is_executable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex A B cid p #s #i.
       Sent(A, B, cid, p) @ #s
     & Accepted(B, A, cid, p) @ #i
     & #s < #i"

/*  THE ONE POSITIVE PROPERTY.  A message accepted over the bearer carries a
    ceremony-id that BOTH devices independently derived -- so the anchor
    cannot be a value the adversary chose, and cannot be one only a single
    party ever computed.  That is ceremony-binding, and it is all of it.

    MUTATION-TESTED: replace `Eq(anchor, cid)` in `Bearer_Take` with a
    tautology and this falsifies, the adversary then supplying an anchor
    nobody derived.                                                         */
lemma an_accepted_anchor_was_derived_by_both:
  "All B A cid p #i.
     Accepted(B, A, cid, p) @ #i
     ==> (Ex #x. Derived(A, B, cid) @ #x & #x < #i)
       & (Ex #y. Derived(B, A, cid) @ #y & #y < #i)"

/*  THE FIRST BOUNDARY, and it VERIFIES because the attack is reachable.  The
    contribution is public (§14.4.1), so an adversary that read the screens
    echoes it correctly and the receiver accepts a payload the sender never
    sent.  §14.4.2 says the echo catches "a bearer that does not agree with
    the screen"; what it catches is a bearer contradicting the screen, never
    one quoting it.                                                          */
lemma the_anchor_does_not_authenticate_the_sender:
  exists-trace
  "Ex B A cid p #i.
       Accepted(B, A, cid, p) @ #i
     & not(Ex #s. Sent(A, B, cid, p) @ #s)"

/*  THE SECOND BOUNDARY, same cause.  The ceremony-id is public too, so a
    payload from one ceremony is re-anchorable into another: the adversary
    rewraps it under the second ceremony's id and echo, both of which it
    read.  Ceremony-binding holds against a party who did not watch the
    screens and not against one who did.                                     */
lemma a_payload_is_re_anchorable_into_another_ceremony:
  exists-trace
  "Ex A B cid1 cid2 p #s #i.
       Sent(A, B, cid1, p) @ #s
     & Accepted(B, A, cid2, p) @ #i
     & not(cid1 = cid2)"

end
